The backend is still complaining that environment is null because the browser is likely caching the old Javascript bundle, or the file in src/main/resources was not updated.

Instead of fighting the minified Javascript, let's fix the backend code so it handles a null environment gracefully by defaulting to "DEV". This is much safer and more reliable!

Step 1: Run this PowerShell command to search for CostRequest.java
This will help us find where the request model is located in your office project:

powershell
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter "CostRequest.java"
Also find CostExplorerDev.java:

powershell
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter "CostExplorerDev.java"
Step 2: Safe Backend Fix
Once we locate CostRequest.java (usually under src/main/java/.../CostRequest.java), we can update the getter to default to "DEV" if it's null.

For example, in CostRequest.java:

java
public String getEnvironment() {
    return environment != null ? environment : "DEV";
}
Or in CostExplorerDev.java at line 45 (or CostContorller.java before calling start):

java
if (request.getEnvironment() == null) {
    request.setEnvironment("DEV");
}
